N={}
N[(1,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a group project (our analogy) where your grade depends on a teammate who has no time and no laptop. Sending reminders will not help; lending your laptop might. Supply chains work the same way. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"scs\">Supply chain sustainability</b> means taking <b>people, planet and profit</b> (the triple P, after Elkington, 1997) into account during a product's whole journey: raw materials, production, storage, delivery and transport. A firm's sustainability therefore depends on partners that may lack the expertise, know-how, means or motivation to change."),
("Why it is hard","The authors give three reasons in their plain language summary: several players have to change their behaviour, it is generally costly, and people lack the resources to make a difference. And most management of it is <b>demand-focused</b>: regulations, policies, managerial decisions. On its own that is often ineffective, because the employees who must act lack the resources. <i>In plain words:</i> rules from the top, on their own, often do not change what happens at the supplier's desk."),
("The idea of the article","Change between organizations does not happen through top-down pressure alone; it needs <b>change agents</b> where sourcing decisions are made: the border between organizations. People who work there are <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"bspan\">boundary spanners</b>; the motivated ones are <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"champ\">sustainability champions</b>. Central claim: a champion should improve the <b>partner's</b> balance of sustainability demands and resources, and so indirectly the own balance, because both sides are linked. The authors do not throw out top-down models; they add a bottom-up route. The framework is <b>triadic</b>: three parts in every sustainability decision, the initiating actor, the partner, and the exchange of demands and resources between their organizations."),
("What the article adds","Three contributions. <b>To supply chain research:</b> a behavioural, bottom-up solution, a possible missing link in a field with little work on individuals. <b>To Job Demands-Resources theory:</b> one employee's crafting can activate an employee in <b>another organization</b>, and this can build into gain or loss spirals. <b>To change research:</b> concrete roles for individuals (the initiator, the resource-providing partner). Table 1 (open it below) shows the micro-level topics studied so far; what employees at the border actually <i>do</i> is not among them. In the T-shirt case (ours): for supply chain research, a buyer's daily choices become the lever; for the theory, the buyer's crafting changes the job of someone at the supplier; for change research, the buyer is the initiator and the supplier's manager the partner."),
("So what","The article is <b>conceptual</b>: it collects no data, it builds a framework with ten propositions for others to test. Keep that in mind for every step: the propositions are proposed, not shown. Next: the building blocks of the framework, sustainability demands and resources."),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a seesaw (our analogy): whatever the person on one end does moves the other. The article's own story: a buyer asks her supplier for more sustainable packaging. At the supplier's desk this is a new demand (develop or buy that packaging), which calls for new resources (know-how, time, money). If the buyer helps, for example with knowledge, her own desk gets a new demand. Two desks, one seesaw: that is the picture behind propositions 2 and 3."),
("New demands, new resources","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"sdem\">Sustainability demands</b> in the article: sourcing new products, extra information, new ways of storage and transport, cultural differences, hazardous working conditions in manufacturing, compliance checks; also time and money pressure. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"sres\">Sustainability resources</b>: capital, sustainability training, autonomy, know-how, a network, managerial support. They come <b>on top of</b> regular duties such as deadlines and quality. <b>Proposition 2:</b> implementing sustainability adds sustainability demands and requires specific sustainability resources, for actors and partners."),
("Tied together","Actor and partner are bound by the exchange between their firms. The article's example: a buyer asks for more sustainable packaging, so the supplier's manager gets a new demand and must find new resources. Expertise is spread over firms, so resources have to be shared. And when demands and resources cross the border they <b>may change in nature</b>: a higher price is a resource for the supplier and (our illustration) a demand on the buyer's budget. <b>Proposition 3:</b> the partners' demands and resources are (inversely) related, so a change on one side prompts an update on the other."),
("So what","The theory was mostly applied to one person at a time. Studies of colleagues showed the link already: when one employee's hindering demands, such as administrative tasks, decreased, a close colleague who took them over got more work (Tims and colleagues, 2015; Tims &amp; Parker, 2020). The authors move this to two people in <b>different</b> organizations who negotiate the costs. Next: what such a demand does inside the partner."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a teacher who raises the bar (our analogy). With tutoring and good books, students rise to it; with only extra tests and penalties, some start to cheat. Job Demands-Resources theory names two routes. In the <b>motivational process</b>, resources build motivation and better performance. In the <b>health-impairment process</b>, chronic demands drain people, causing strain and worse performance. What decides is the <b>balance</b>: resources soften the drain of demands (the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"buffer\">buffer hypothesis</b>). Proposition 4 is the motivational process and proposition 5 the impairment process, both running inside the partner."),
("The motivational route","A demand from the initiator costs the partner resources. With <b>high</b> access to sustainability resources the drain is buffered: the partner becomes more motivated and shows more sustainability-promoting behaviour. That is <b>proposition 4</b>. Two kinds of support. Among colleagues, an employee who raised challenging demands together with resources increased co-workers' work enjoyment and willingness to collaborate (Fong and colleagues, 2022). Among firms, suppliers reduced violations when buyers gave training or price premiums along with codes of conduct and audits (Porteous and colleagues, 2015; Yawar &amp; Seuring, 2018)."),
("The impairment route","With <b>low</b> access to resources, the same demand may lead to strain and to <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"undermine\">sustainability-undermining behaviour</b>: behaviour that creates obstacles, such as hiding practices or audit fraud. That is <b>proposition 5</b>. Many buyers manage exactly this way: codes of conduct, audits, fines or ending the contract. Relying on sticks without carrots often fails in the long term and can push suppliers with few resources to hide information from auditors (Plambeck &amp; Taylor, 2016)."),
("So what","The trigger sits in <b>another organization</b>: the initiator's demand starts the process, the partner's resources decide the route. So the lesson is not \"ask less\" but \"give as well\". The authors hedge with \"may\": these routes are expected, not yet tested between individual employees. Next: what the champion can do about it."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of the oxygen-mask rule on a plane (our analogy): put on your own mask first, then help your neighbour. A sustainability champion does both. It starts with a <b>spark</b>: an individual's own wish to work sustainably, shaped by attitudes, social norms and perceived control, and especially influential when the organization's objectives are unclear (Swaim and colleagues, 2016). But a spark needs tools: autonomy, support, room to grow. <b>Proposition 1:</b> implementation requires a motivated, proactive initiator who can mobilize enough sustainability resources in the own work environment."),
("Helping the partner","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"pfr\">Partner-focused regulation</b> means <b>intentionally</b> changing the demands and resources of a person in another organization: imposing sustainability demands and providing sustainability resources. Firms call this <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"supdev\">supplier development</b>: direct (investments, training) or indirect (certification, auditing). <b>Proposition 6:</b> actors can use it to improve the partner's balance and trigger the partner's motivation and promoting behaviour. To do it well they need <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"metak\">meta-knowledge</b>: knowing what the partner can handle and lacks."),
("It costs","What you give, you no longer have. Resources provided to the partner come out of the actor's own pool; crafting also takes energy and can bring role conflict, disappointment and regret in the short term, and the payoff of crafting someone else's job is even less certain. <b>Proposition 7:</b> partner-focused regulation may draw on the actor's own resources, risking an initial disbalance. <i>In plain words:</i> helping the supplier can hurt your own targets at first."),
("Own balance first","Buyers often lack autonomy, managerial support, training, capital and room to decide, and their sustainability effort is voluntary, so the organization does not replace what they spend. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"sfr\">Self-focused regulation</b> repairs this: <b>optimizing</b> own demands and <b>seeking</b> own resources. Why first? <b>Conservation of resources theory</b> (Hobfoll, 1989), which you met in theme 1: people with few resources protect what they have; only with a starting pool do they invest. <b>Proposition 8:</b> self-focused regulation optimizes the actor's own balance so that partner-focused regulation becomes possible. An earlier study found these behaviours went with more daily proactive sustainability behaviour (Pekaar &amp; Demerouti, 2023). <b>So what:</b> four strategies, two for me and two for my partner. Next: what happens over time."),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a tennis rally (our analogy): if both keep returning playable balls the rally grows; if one only smashes, the other starts missing and the game falls apart. Job Demands-Resources theory already has loops inside one person: demands and resources, proactive behaviour and outcomes strengthen each other over time, for better (gain) or worse (loss) (Bakker &amp; Demerouti, 2018). The article moves these loops <b>between</b> two people in different organizations. Sustainability, as the authors put it citing Bouchery and colleagues (2024), is a journey, not a destination."),
("The gain spiral","Two kinds of demands matter here. <b>Challenging</b> demands stretch people and can motivate; <b>hindering</b> demands only obstruct. In a gain spiral the actor raises challenging demands and removes hindering ones through support. By the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"boost\">boosting hypothesis</b>, a challenging demand makes the partner's resources more valuable and prompts a motivated search for more. The partner then shares what it finds, so resources come back to the initiator. <b>Proposition 9:</b> a balanced resource exchange is related to a gain spiral in which the initiator's sharing of resources and the partner's promoting behaviour strengthen each other over time. The authors cite reading 5 (Luu, 2019) as an example inside organizations, with support from colleagues and managers as the active mechanism, and add that in today's resource-poor supply chains gain spirals are <b>not likely</b>."),
("The loss spiral","People with few resources, by conservation of resources theory, defend what they have; under strain they use poor strategies (poor communication, careless mistakes, conflict) that add to their demands. Among colleagues, crafting driven by avoiding losses reduced the partner's cooperation and raised conflict (Fong and colleagues, 2022). Between firms: demands without resources push the partner toward undermining behaviour and the initiator toward sharing less. Suppliers audited by many buyers can develop <b>audit fatigue</b>, which distracts from real improvement and may end in audit fraud. <b>Proposition 10:</b> an unbalanced exchange (high demands, low resources) is related to a loss spiral of low sharing and undermining behaviour."),
("Longer chains","The framework describes a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"dyad\">dyad</b>, but it is <b>reversible</b>: a motivated partner can become the initiator toward its own supplier. So people in the chain have a dual role, meeting demands from above and passing demands and resources on. The result can ripple up and down the chain. Fitting finding: first-tier suppliers' resources decide whether they can drive sustainability to second-tier suppliers (Wilhelm and colleagues, 2016)."),
("How to test it","The propositions are a research agenda. Collect data from <b>both sides</b> of the pair. Choose the time frame: a diary or event-sampling study (short questions every day or after each event) for quick reactions, a longitudinal panel (the same people measured a few times, months apart) for slow ones; the spirals need several measurements. Analyse with <b>Actor-Partner Interdependence Models</b>, which separate what my behaviour does for me from what it does for my partner, or let a computer simulate the loops (agent-based modelling, system dynamics). <b>So what:</b> next, the whole framework in one view."),
]
